WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.913 --> 00:00:11.330
CHRISTOPHER SANSON:
Hey, everyone.

00:00:11.330 --> 00:00:14.260
Welcome to Dev 301,
production-grade

00:00:14.260 --> 00:00:16.910
build security for
developer workflows.

00:00:16.910 --> 00:00:18.580
My name is Christopher Sanson.

00:00:18.580 --> 00:00:21.460
I'm a Product Manager at Google
working on developer tooling.

00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:25.090
And with me is Anthony Bushong,
Specialist Customer Engineer

00:00:25.090 --> 00:00:27.820
who will be joining us a little
bit later to give a demo about

00:00:27.820 --> 00:00:29.410
some of the new
features and concepts

00:00:29.410 --> 00:00:32.780
we'll be talking about.

00:00:32.780 --> 00:00:35.240
The increase in cyber
and ransomware attacks

00:00:35.240 --> 00:00:37.610
over the last year,
including high profile cases

00:00:37.610 --> 00:00:40.760
such as SolarWinds, has been a
wake up call for the industry.

00:00:40.760 --> 00:00:43.580
Security is top of mind for
companies large and small

00:00:43.580 --> 00:00:45.530
across all verticals.

00:00:45.530 --> 00:00:47.870
In May, the White House
issued an executive order

00:00:47.870 --> 00:00:50.120
on improving the nation's
cybersecurity in face

00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:52.850
of increasingly sophisticated
and persistent threats

00:00:52.850 --> 00:00:55.832
to the public and
private sectors.

00:00:55.832 --> 00:00:57.290
In this 20 minute
talk, we're going

00:00:57.290 --> 00:00:58.910
to introduce some
specific actions

00:00:58.910 --> 00:01:01.160
that you can take to
create a more secure

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:02.990
build environment
on Google Cloud.

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:07.320
Over the last 20
years, Google has

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:09.330
built some of the most
secure computing systems

00:01:09.330 --> 00:01:13.080
in the world, pioneering
concepts such as zero trust.

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:16.170
As part of GCP, we're bringing
these internal best practices

00:01:16.170 --> 00:01:18.900
to our cloud customers
through both open standards

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:22.380
and integration in a variety
of ways into GCP's managed

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:24.210
services.

00:01:24.210 --> 00:01:27.120
In June of this year, we
launched Open Source Insights,

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:28.950
an exploratory
visualization site

00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:31.620
for viewing the dependencies
of open source projects.

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:34.800
And in July, we
announced SLSA, S-L-S-A,

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:38.460
an open framework based on
internal Google best practices

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:41.850
for formalizing the criteria
around software supply chain

00:01:41.850 --> 00:01:45.180
integrity to help the industry
and open source ecosystem

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:47.220
secure the software
development lifecycle.

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:53.820
CI/CD is one of the most
critical paths for securing

00:01:53.820 --> 00:01:55.500
your software supply chain.

00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:58.170
It's the gatekeeper by which
code changes are released out

00:01:58.170 --> 00:02:01.500
to your users and has many entry
points for vulnerabilities,

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:03.780
pulling resources
from a large number

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:07.080
of internal and external sources
while accepting contributions

00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:09.449
from hundreds or maybe even
thousands of developers

00:02:09.449 --> 00:02:12.250
and other contributors
at your company.

00:02:12.250 --> 00:02:14.920
In the SolarWinds breach, that,
in part, led to the White House

00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:18.310
executive order, it was actually
a compromise built system

00:02:18.310 --> 00:02:21.520
that attackers used to inject
malware into the candidate

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:24.370
release that was ultimately
published to downstream users.

00:02:26.643 --> 00:02:28.310
So in this talk, we're
going to focus in

00:02:28.310 --> 00:02:30.680
on the build process
specifically and talk

00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:33.110
about how to improve the
security posture of your built

00:02:33.110 --> 00:02:34.040
environment.

00:02:34.040 --> 00:02:37.050
And we're going to do this by
focusing on three main areas.

00:02:37.050 --> 00:02:38.690
One, network security.

00:02:38.690 --> 00:02:40.370
Two, build permissions.

00:02:40.370 --> 00:02:44.780
And three, binary authorization.

00:02:44.780 --> 00:02:47.330
So let's start with
network security.

00:02:47.330 --> 00:02:48.980
One of the risks
to an organization

00:02:48.980 --> 00:02:51.650
is data exfiltration
at build time.

00:02:51.650 --> 00:02:53.540
Built by their nature
typically handle

00:02:53.540 --> 00:02:55.880
a large amount of private
intellectual property

00:02:55.880 --> 00:03:00.223
across source code, databases
and artifact repositories.

00:03:00.223 --> 00:03:01.640
Now, actually, we
want to minimize

00:03:01.640 --> 00:03:05.280
the risk of any of this data
egress to the public internet,

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:07.970
whether it happens to be
maliciously or just simply

00:03:07.970 --> 00:03:09.340
accidentally.

00:03:09.340 --> 00:03:11.090
The most common way
that companies do this

00:03:11.090 --> 00:03:13.580
is by hosting resources
inside their private network

00:03:13.580 --> 00:03:15.713
behind a firewall.

00:03:15.713 --> 00:03:17.130
Traditionally,
this has meant then

00:03:17.130 --> 00:03:20.490
that you are self hosting
your own CI/CD solution.

00:03:20.490 --> 00:03:23.310
And besides just the additional
overhead and total cost

00:03:23.310 --> 00:03:26.340
of ownership around
developer velocity of having

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:29.670
to install, manage, upgrade
and scale this system,

00:03:29.670 --> 00:03:32.790
it also means that you're
responsible for securing it.

00:03:32.790 --> 00:03:34.950
And as we saw with
the SolarWinds attack,

00:03:34.950 --> 00:03:39.630
bad actors can try to corrupt or
attack the build system itself.

00:03:39.630 --> 00:03:43.980
In the case of SolarWinds,
where at build time,

00:03:43.980 --> 00:03:46.230
when a file was fetched
from the source repo,

00:03:46.230 --> 00:03:48.990
it was actually swapped
out with malicious code

00:03:48.990 --> 00:03:51.750
and then sent to downstream
users through the compile,

00:03:51.750 --> 00:03:53.220
build and publish process.

00:03:57.170 --> 00:03:58.720
So let's take a
different approach.

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:00.970
Fully managed solutions,
like Google's Cloud Build,

00:04:00.970 --> 00:04:03.490
offer a different tactic
and are hosted and managed

00:04:03.490 --> 00:04:06.280
by Google Cloud running in
Google's secure network,

00:04:06.280 --> 00:04:08.380
taking the burden off
of you and your team

00:04:08.380 --> 00:04:11.350
for having to secure the system
itself in terms of accessing

00:04:11.350 --> 00:04:14.530
the workers that builds run on.

00:04:14.530 --> 00:04:17.959
In June, Cloud Build launched
Private Pools, a new feature

00:04:17.959 --> 00:04:20.170
which lets you securely
connect a fully managed

00:04:20.170 --> 00:04:23.310
build system into your private
network using VPC network

00:04:23.310 --> 00:04:24.280
peering.

00:04:24.280 --> 00:04:27.850
It supports private IPs on the
worker, data regionalization

00:04:27.850 --> 00:04:32.360
and static IP ranges
for IP allow listing.

00:04:32.360 --> 00:04:35.210
Another common pattern
with this firewall approach

00:04:35.210 --> 00:04:37.710
alone is that, in
many organizations,

00:04:37.710 --> 00:04:39.650
it's treated as all or nothing.

00:04:39.650 --> 00:04:42.230
There's inside the firewall
and outside the firewall.

00:04:42.230 --> 00:04:45.200
Then once inside, you can
access anything that is also

00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:46.712
inside the private network.

00:04:46.712 --> 00:04:48.920
Now, one of the best practices
that we want to follow

00:04:48.920 --> 00:04:50.420
is that builds can
access only what

00:04:50.420 --> 00:04:52.692
they need to and nothing more.

00:04:52.692 --> 00:04:54.150
So let's say, for
example, that you

00:04:54.150 --> 00:04:56.700
have resources in
multiple GCP projects

00:04:56.700 --> 00:04:58.492
that your build needs to access.

00:04:58.492 --> 00:04:59.950
How could we
configure Cloud Builds

00:04:59.950 --> 00:05:02.250
so that it can access
these resources, but only

00:05:02.250 --> 00:05:04.440
these resources and
nothing else that would

00:05:04.440 --> 00:05:06.105
be sort of an attack factor?

00:05:08.970 --> 00:05:11.180
So this is where VPC
Service Controls come in.

00:05:11.180 --> 00:05:14.450
With Private Pool support
for VPC Service Controls,

00:05:14.450 --> 00:05:16.220
you can configure
Cloud Build to restrict

00:05:16.220 --> 00:05:18.710
access more granularly
to just the services

00:05:18.710 --> 00:05:20.930
that it needs to access.

00:05:20.930 --> 00:05:24.280
Additionally, you can use VPC
Service Controls on the project

00:05:24.280 --> 00:05:27.880
that the worker pool itself
is in to restrict egress

00:05:27.880 --> 00:05:29.710
and an additional
layer of security

00:05:29.710 --> 00:05:33.610
against leakage of data
to the public internet.

00:05:33.610 --> 00:05:37.180
Using Private Pool's support
for the GCP org policy,

00:05:37.180 --> 00:05:38.800
you can enforce
that all builds are

00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:41.350
run on these approved worker
pools that were successfully

00:05:41.350 --> 00:05:42.790
set up by your security team.

00:05:47.510 --> 00:05:49.240
So now we talked about
network security,

00:05:49.240 --> 00:05:51.190
let's move over to
build permissions

00:05:51.190 --> 00:05:52.990
where we have two main goals.

00:05:52.990 --> 00:05:55.570
Again, we want to grant builds
only the permissions they need

00:05:55.570 --> 00:05:57.040
and nothing more.

00:05:57.040 --> 00:06:00.550
And second, we want to avoid
the escalation of permissions

00:06:00.550 --> 00:06:02.640
from the build
system to developers.

00:06:02.640 --> 00:06:05.140
We don't want developers to be
able to access our production

00:06:05.140 --> 00:06:08.890
environments directly or decrypt
production secrets that the

00:06:08.890 --> 00:06:11.140
build system needs but
developers shouldn't really

00:06:11.140 --> 00:06:13.730
have direct access to.

00:06:13.730 --> 00:06:17.450
So as an example, let's say we
have two environments, staging

00:06:17.450 --> 00:06:18.895
and production.

00:06:18.895 --> 00:06:20.270
The first thing
we're going to do

00:06:20.270 --> 00:06:23.040
is enforce that all changes
go through our source

00:06:23.040 --> 00:06:26.210
repo, or version control
system, which then invokes

00:06:26.210 --> 00:06:27.980
the build automatically.

00:06:27.980 --> 00:06:33.830
This has a number of benefits,
including change history,

00:06:33.830 --> 00:06:35.870
audit trail, CI tests.

00:06:35.870 --> 00:06:38.720
And using productive
branches, we

00:06:38.720 --> 00:06:42.380
can enforce that only developers
that have commit rights

00:06:42.380 --> 00:06:44.270
to the prod branch
have the ability

00:06:44.270 --> 00:06:47.740
to invoke the production
release pipeline.

00:06:47.740 --> 00:06:51.250
This lets us use pull
requests to add a approval

00:06:51.250 --> 00:06:53.980
or manual review layer
to all suggested changes

00:06:53.980 --> 00:06:57.430
from developers that don't have
permission to release directly

00:06:57.430 --> 00:06:58.711
to production.

00:07:01.360 --> 00:07:03.760
Likewise, we're going
to want to make sure

00:07:03.760 --> 00:07:07.413
that the staging build doesn't
have permissions to access

00:07:07.413 --> 00:07:09.580
our production environment
either, which would allow

00:07:09.580 --> 00:07:11.230
another escalation
of permissions

00:07:11.230 --> 00:07:14.050
to environments and
secrets that should not

00:07:14.050 --> 00:07:16.365
be accessible from
our staging pipeline.

00:07:16.365 --> 00:07:17.990
For that, we're going
to take advantage

00:07:17.990 --> 00:07:20.870
of a new feature of Cloud
Build for user-provided service

00:07:20.870 --> 00:07:21.715
accounts.

00:07:21.715 --> 00:07:23.840
In this instance, we're
going to create two service

00:07:23.840 --> 00:07:25.730
accounts, one for
our staging bill

00:07:25.730 --> 00:07:28.400
and one for our production
bill, and grant them

00:07:28.400 --> 00:07:31.340
the respective permissions that
they need so that they can only

00:07:31.340 --> 00:07:32.840
access the resources
that are needed

00:07:32.840 --> 00:07:34.230
in each of those environments.

00:07:36.827 --> 00:07:38.910
Next, we're going to use
Cloud Build's integration

00:07:38.910 --> 00:07:42.330
with Secret Manager for
managing the secrets.

00:07:42.330 --> 00:07:46.260
With Cloud Build's native
Secret Manager integration,

00:07:46.260 --> 00:07:48.430
secrets are only
decrypted at build time,

00:07:48.430 --> 00:07:51.180
and so not available in plain
text in the build config

00:07:51.180 --> 00:07:52.530
or to developers.

00:07:52.530 --> 00:07:54.360
And because you're
using Secret Manager,

00:07:54.360 --> 00:07:57.630
it's much easier and faster
to rotate secrets frequently

00:07:57.630 --> 00:07:59.400
further, improving
your security posture.

00:08:03.080 --> 00:08:05.780
The last thing we're going to
do is add a manual approval step

00:08:05.780 --> 00:08:07.250
for the production pipeline.

00:08:07.250 --> 00:08:09.770
Using Cloud Build's
approval features,

00:08:09.770 --> 00:08:12.200
we are going to make
sure that an admin

00:08:12.200 --> 00:08:14.900
or other approved reviewer
is able to manually

00:08:14.900 --> 00:08:18.499
review and approve all
production deployments.

00:08:22.810 --> 00:08:24.430
And all of this is
in the spirit of,

00:08:24.430 --> 00:08:27.970
again, preventing access
from individual developers

00:08:27.970 --> 00:08:30.002
to production environments
and other secrets

00:08:30.002 --> 00:08:31.960
that they should not be
able to access directly

00:08:31.960 --> 00:08:36.417
while enabling the build
system to deploy to production.

00:08:39.043 --> 00:08:41.210
So now we've talked about
build permissions as well.

00:08:41.210 --> 00:08:45.150
Let's move on to the last topic,
which is Binary Authorization.

00:08:45.150 --> 00:08:47.610
So we've created our
built pipeline that builds

00:08:47.610 --> 00:08:50.490
our container and deploys it
out-- in this particular case,

00:08:50.490 --> 00:08:52.620
Kubernetes Engine or Cloud Run--

00:08:52.620 --> 00:08:54.990
through our continuous
delivery pipeline,

00:08:54.990 --> 00:08:58.513
whether that could be the
newly announced Cloud Deploy,

00:08:58.513 --> 00:09:00.930
an open source solution like
Spinnaker or even Cloud Build

00:09:00.930 --> 00:09:02.730
itself.

00:09:02.730 --> 00:09:06.450
But how do we enforce that only
approved builds are deployed

00:09:06.450 --> 00:09:07.800
to our runtime environments?

00:09:07.800 --> 00:09:10.410
What's stopping a bad
or unauthorized actor

00:09:10.410 --> 00:09:12.540
from building their
own image and then

00:09:12.540 --> 00:09:14.730
deploying that out
directly or injecting it

00:09:14.730 --> 00:09:16.307
into our CD pipeline?

00:09:18.930 --> 00:09:21.210
For this we're going to
leverage Binary Authorization

00:09:21.210 --> 00:09:23.880
from GCP, which lets
you define deployment

00:09:23.880 --> 00:09:27.580
policies for which containers
can and can't be deployed.

00:09:27.580 --> 00:09:29.430
So in this case, let's
create a policy that

00:09:29.430 --> 00:09:31.740
says only containers
built by Cloud Build

00:09:31.740 --> 00:09:35.770
can be deployed out
to GKE or Cloud Run.

00:09:35.770 --> 00:09:38.740
This will ensure that any
unsigned images or images

00:09:38.740 --> 00:09:42.340
signed by an untrusted build
system will fail our policy

00:09:42.340 --> 00:09:45.890
check and not get deployed
out to our production users,

00:09:45.890 --> 00:09:48.850
whether trying to be deployed
directly or through our CD

00:09:48.850 --> 00:09:52.040
pipeline.

00:09:52.040 --> 00:09:55.970
Lastly, Cloud Build will create
and assign an asset station

00:09:55.970 --> 00:09:58.550
for each image that
it builds saying

00:09:58.550 --> 00:10:00.020
that it was built
by Cloud Build,

00:10:00.020 --> 00:10:02.562
allowing it to pass the binary
authorization and verification

00:10:02.562 --> 00:10:05.898
process and get deployed
out to our environment.

00:10:10.200 --> 00:10:12.447
So we talked quickly
about Cloud Build

00:10:12.447 --> 00:10:14.780
and how you can use Cloud
Build to improve your security

00:10:14.780 --> 00:10:17.240
posture to create a more
secure build environment while,

00:10:17.240 --> 00:10:19.520
at the same time, improving
developer productivity

00:10:19.520 --> 00:10:22.820
and reducing operational
overhead altogether.

00:10:22.820 --> 00:10:25.130
So we talked about some of
the features that were newly

00:10:25.130 --> 00:10:28.070
launched in 2021,
including Private Pools,

00:10:28.070 --> 00:10:32.060
user-provided service accounts,
Secret Manager integration,

00:10:32.060 --> 00:10:34.850
manual approvals
and better support

00:10:34.850 --> 00:10:36.170
for Binary Authorization.

00:10:38.825 --> 00:10:40.200
Now, let's hand
it off to Anthony

00:10:40.200 --> 00:10:42.510
for a demo of some
of what we discussed.

00:10:42.510 --> 00:10:43.638
Anthony.

00:10:43.638 --> 00:10:45.180
ANTHONY BUSHONG:
Thanks, Christopher.

00:10:45.180 --> 00:10:49.593
Let's dive into how we can put
these principles into practice.

00:10:49.593 --> 00:10:52.010
Let's start by taking a look
at our Cloud Run application.

00:10:52.010 --> 00:10:54.980
In this case, we're utilizing a
containerised Python Flask web

00:10:54.980 --> 00:10:57.260
app that's accessible
at this URL.

00:10:57.260 --> 00:10:59.640
And it's only accessible
by authorized users,

00:10:59.640 --> 00:11:02.750
so it will provide a token
when issuing a request.

00:11:02.750 --> 00:11:05.780
And you'll see that we get
a HTML page in response

00:11:05.780 --> 00:11:08.420
to our request.

00:11:08.420 --> 00:11:10.100
Our Cloud Run
application actually

00:11:10.100 --> 00:11:12.440
has Binary
Authorization enabled.

00:11:12.440 --> 00:11:14.330
As a reminder,
Binary Authorization

00:11:14.330 --> 00:11:18.770
is the ability to only admit a
certain set of container images

00:11:18.770 --> 00:11:22.640
to your runtime environment,
like Cloud Run or GKE.

00:11:22.640 --> 00:11:25.040
In this case, we've configured
Binary Authorization

00:11:25.040 --> 00:11:28.940
to only approve images that have
been signed by the Cloud Run

00:11:28.940 --> 00:11:29.810
attestor.

00:11:29.810 --> 00:11:31.670
In this case, the
Cloud Run attestor

00:11:31.670 --> 00:11:33.530
is only signing
images that have been

00:11:33.530 --> 00:11:37.010
built with Google Cloud Build.

00:11:37.010 --> 00:11:39.560
We can see Binary
Authorization in action

00:11:39.560 --> 00:11:43.010
if we try to deploy an image
to the Cloud Run service that

00:11:43.010 --> 00:11:45.530
was not signed by our tester.

00:11:45.530 --> 00:11:48.433
So in this case, for
our next 2021 app,

00:11:48.433 --> 00:11:50.600
you'll see here that I have
a container image that's

00:11:50.600 --> 00:11:53.180
tagged rogue, and this
was built out of band,

00:11:53.180 --> 00:11:55.940
not utilizing
Google Cloud Build.

00:11:55.940 --> 00:11:58.490
And if I was to try to deploy
this container image to Cloud

00:11:58.490 --> 00:12:03.110
Run, we should actually see
our Binary Authorization policy

00:12:03.110 --> 00:12:05.480
in action by denying
that container image

00:12:05.480 --> 00:12:08.300
to be deployed to Cloud Run.

00:12:08.300 --> 00:12:10.550
Now that we've demonstrated
how we can handle images

00:12:10.550 --> 00:12:12.230
that we don't trust,
let's take a look

00:12:12.230 --> 00:12:14.150
at the automation that
Cloud Build provides

00:12:14.150 --> 00:12:16.910
and how we can build
images that we do trust.

00:12:16.910 --> 00:12:19.970
To build our container image,
what we're actually utilizing

00:12:19.970 --> 00:12:23.270
are Google Cloud Build's
support for triggers.

00:12:23.270 --> 00:12:24.860
Both of these
triggers are connected

00:12:24.860 --> 00:12:28.100
to my GitHub repository titled
Next 2021, which actually

00:12:28.100 --> 00:12:30.200
has our application code.

00:12:30.200 --> 00:12:33.170
The first trigger kicks
off builds automatically

00:12:33.170 --> 00:12:35.960
based on any changes
to a feature branch.

00:12:35.960 --> 00:12:39.860
In this case, it'll run builds
that build a container image,

00:12:39.860 --> 00:12:42.320
signs a container image,
and will actually open up

00:12:42.320 --> 00:12:44.570
a pull request against the
main branch in the GitHub

00:12:44.570 --> 00:12:46.340
repository.

00:12:46.340 --> 00:12:48.080
The second trigger
actually kicks off

00:12:48.080 --> 00:12:50.930
and deploys the container
image to Cloud Run

00:12:50.930 --> 00:12:54.760
based on any changes
made to the main branch.

00:12:54.760 --> 00:12:56.510
One of the things we
want to consider here

00:12:56.510 --> 00:12:59.840
is the ability to apply the
principle of least privilege.

00:12:59.840 --> 00:13:01.910
And in this case, we're
actually able to do this

00:13:01.910 --> 00:13:04.250
by separating the service
accounts that each of these

00:13:04.250 --> 00:13:05.433
triggers utilize.

00:13:05.433 --> 00:13:07.100
You can see here, for
the first trigger,

00:13:07.100 --> 00:13:08.475
we're utilizing
a service account

00:13:08.475 --> 00:13:10.807
that is titled build-next-2021.

00:13:10.807 --> 00:13:12.890
And this has permissions
to the Container Registry

00:13:12.890 --> 00:13:14.307
and things like
that that actually

00:13:14.307 --> 00:13:17.642
help it to perform the
build that it needs to run.

00:13:17.642 --> 00:13:19.100
And then the second
service account

00:13:19.100 --> 00:13:22.100
is called deploy-next-2021,
which has permissions

00:13:22.100 --> 00:13:24.800
to deploy to Cloud Run.

00:13:24.800 --> 00:13:27.320
While in this example I'm
utilizing a GitHub.com

00:13:27.320 --> 00:13:29.540
repository, it is
important to call out

00:13:29.540 --> 00:13:33.770
that Google Cloud Build also has
support for self-hosted source

00:13:33.770 --> 00:13:35.730
code management systems
like GitHub Enterprise.

00:13:35.730 --> 00:13:37.730
So you can actually see
here that in Cloud Build

00:13:37.730 --> 00:13:40.280
I actually have a host
connection to a private GitHub

00:13:40.280 --> 00:13:44.215
Enterprise server that I
also utilize in Google Cloud.

00:13:44.215 --> 00:13:46.340
And you can see that I've
actually made connections

00:13:46.340 --> 00:13:50.360
to various repositories in that
GitHub Enterprise instance.

00:13:50.360 --> 00:13:51.920
This means that
I can also create

00:13:51.920 --> 00:13:54.620
Cloud Build triggers on
these private self-hosted

00:13:54.620 --> 00:13:56.460
repositories.

00:13:56.460 --> 00:13:57.920
Now let's take a
look at the actual

00:13:57.920 --> 00:14:01.250
build that our first
trigger is running.

00:14:01.250 --> 00:14:03.283
This trigger is looking
in the build folder.

00:14:03.283 --> 00:14:04.700
And the first
trigger specifically

00:14:04.700 --> 00:14:08.420
is looking at the build.yaml
specification for Google Cloud

00:14:08.420 --> 00:14:09.255
Build.

00:14:09.255 --> 00:14:11.630
Our first build step is building
our container image that

00:14:11.630 --> 00:14:13.243
will end up running
in Cloud Run,

00:14:13.243 --> 00:14:14.660
and we're actually
publishing that

00:14:14.660 --> 00:14:16.700
to Google Container Registry.

00:14:16.700 --> 00:14:18.950
And you can see here that
we're utilizing Cloud Native

00:14:18.950 --> 00:14:22.677
Buildpacks to automatically
generate that container image.

00:14:22.677 --> 00:14:24.260
Once we've signed
the container image,

00:14:24.260 --> 00:14:27.470
our final build step actually
will open up a pull request

00:14:27.470 --> 00:14:29.370
against our GitHub repository.

00:14:29.370 --> 00:14:31.970
And you can see here that the
way that we're able to do this

00:14:31.970 --> 00:14:34.430
is that we're utilizing
the GitHub API.

00:14:34.430 --> 00:14:36.290
And in order to
authorize ourselves,

00:14:36.290 --> 00:14:38.810
or the build, to be able
to perform this action,

00:14:38.810 --> 00:14:42.110
we're providing a GitHub token.

00:14:42.110 --> 00:14:45.680
And so one of the critical
components of a secure

00:14:45.680 --> 00:14:49.250
build environment is
careful secret management.

00:14:49.250 --> 00:14:51.050
In this case, Google
Cloud Build allows

00:14:51.050 --> 00:14:55.610
us to natively access secrets
stored in Google Cloud Secret

00:14:55.610 --> 00:14:56.450
Manager.

00:14:56.450 --> 00:14:58.430
So you can see in this
scenario we're actually

00:14:58.430 --> 00:15:01.880
storing our token, our GitHub
token, in Secret Manager

00:15:01.880 --> 00:15:05.390
and providing that back to the
build itself as an environment

00:15:05.390 --> 00:15:06.210
variable.

00:15:06.210 --> 00:15:09.740
This means that we are not
providing static credentials

00:15:09.740 --> 00:15:12.290
to our actual build
or codifying that

00:15:12.290 --> 00:15:14.930
into our build of
container images,

00:15:14.930 --> 00:15:17.030
but rather these are
dynamically accessed

00:15:17.030 --> 00:15:21.978
utilizing the service account,
which is provided to the build.

00:15:21.978 --> 00:15:24.020
If we jump over to Google
Cloud's Secret Manager,

00:15:24.020 --> 00:15:25.760
we'll see our secret,
our GitHub token.

00:15:25.760 --> 00:15:27.260
And we'll see here
under permissions

00:15:27.260 --> 00:15:30.470
that we've actually granted
secret access or permissions

00:15:30.470 --> 00:15:33.620
to our build-next-2021 service
account, which is being

00:15:33.620 --> 00:15:35.087
utilized by Google Cloud Build.

00:15:35.087 --> 00:15:36.920
And the important thing
to note is that this

00:15:36.920 --> 00:15:38.140
is a granular permission.

00:15:38.140 --> 00:15:40.640
It is not for all secrets that
are stored in Secret Manager,

00:15:40.640 --> 00:15:44.688
but rather on a per
secret access basis.

00:15:44.688 --> 00:15:46.730
The next important field
to call our attention to

00:15:46.730 --> 00:15:52.020
in the Cloud Build specification
is under options worker pool.

00:15:52.020 --> 00:15:54.600
This actually allows for
us to specify on a per

00:15:54.600 --> 00:15:58.410
build basis whether or not a
build should run in a Cloud

00:15:58.410 --> 00:16:00.460
Build private pool.

00:16:00.460 --> 00:16:03.060
So what is a Cloud
Build private pool?

00:16:03.060 --> 00:16:06.210
Well, private pools extend
the user configurability

00:16:06.210 --> 00:16:09.870
for the workers that
actually run a user's build.

00:16:09.870 --> 00:16:13.260
So in this case, we can specify
additional things like regions.

00:16:13.260 --> 00:16:16.625
We can also specify and have
access to more machine types.

00:16:16.625 --> 00:16:18.000
And perhaps, most
importantly, we

00:16:18.000 --> 00:16:21.240
have private VPC connectivity
to access private resources,

00:16:21.240 --> 00:16:23.460
either within our own
network in Google Cloud

00:16:23.460 --> 00:16:26.970
or within the data center that
is connected to our Google

00:16:26.970 --> 00:16:28.350
Cloud VPC.

00:16:28.350 --> 00:16:30.180
So you can see here
that I have specified

00:16:30.180 --> 00:16:32.970
for sandbox-private pool
to have connectivity

00:16:32.970 --> 00:16:36.073
to sandbox-VPC-privatepools,
which is actually running

00:16:36.073 --> 00:16:38.490
in the GitHub Enterprise server
that I was showing earlier

00:16:38.490 --> 00:16:40.620
in this demonstration.

00:16:40.620 --> 00:16:42.960
Not only do we get
private VPC connectivity

00:16:42.960 --> 00:16:45.060
with Cloud Build private
pools, we actually

00:16:45.060 --> 00:16:47.580
have the ability to remove
external IPs from our workers

00:16:47.580 --> 00:16:48.670
altogether.

00:16:48.670 --> 00:16:50.340
And in general, can
now consider how

00:16:50.340 --> 00:16:54.120
we can use VPC Service Controls
to secure our build perimeter

00:16:54.120 --> 00:16:56.520
running in private pools.

00:16:56.520 --> 00:16:58.800
So now let's actually
trigger this first

00:16:58.800 --> 00:17:01.500
build that'll run
on a private pool.

00:17:01.500 --> 00:17:03.690
So I'm going to change
the web application

00:17:03.690 --> 00:17:07.079
and just add a couple of
small, very minor changes

00:17:07.079 --> 00:17:09.119
by adding a couple
of exclamation points

00:17:09.119 --> 00:17:11.220
in some of the HTML.

00:17:11.220 --> 00:17:13.615
And I'm going to commit this
to the development branch,

00:17:13.615 --> 00:17:15.240
and then what we're
going to want to do

00:17:15.240 --> 00:17:17.849
is create a feature branch that
will kick off our trigger now

00:17:17.849 --> 00:17:21.480
that we're done with
the development branch.

00:17:21.480 --> 00:17:23.280
And as you can see,
our build is now

00:17:23.280 --> 00:17:27.067
queued to run in US-west1
on a private pool.

00:17:27.067 --> 00:17:28.650
And if we actually
dig into the build,

00:17:28.650 --> 00:17:31.650
we can see our build steps,
which builds a container image,

00:17:31.650 --> 00:17:34.170
attests a container image,
or signs a container image,

00:17:34.170 --> 00:17:37.140
and then creates that
GitHub pull request.

00:17:37.140 --> 00:17:38.790
And after a short
amount of time,

00:17:38.790 --> 00:17:42.240
we can see that our build has
completed, the final step being

00:17:42.240 --> 00:17:44.020
the pull request being created.

00:17:44.020 --> 00:17:46.450
So if we actually jump
over to the repository,

00:17:46.450 --> 00:17:50.400
we can actually see the PR
from feature against main.

00:17:50.400 --> 00:17:52.470
And so we can review that.

00:17:52.470 --> 00:17:54.270
We can see that our
build is completed,

00:17:54.270 --> 00:17:57.870
and now we can utilize
it as an opportunity

00:17:57.870 --> 00:18:01.890
to basically gate the next build
because we have the opportunity

00:18:01.890 --> 00:18:05.340
to either approve and
merge these changes

00:18:05.340 --> 00:18:08.070
or we can close the PR.

00:18:08.070 --> 00:18:10.260
So in this case, once
we click on merge,

00:18:10.260 --> 00:18:12.840
we're actually going to kick
off the second trigger which

00:18:12.840 --> 00:18:16.920
will deploy the
service to Cloud Run.

00:18:16.920 --> 00:18:20.630
So we're going to click that.

00:18:20.630 --> 00:18:25.100
And that branch has now
been successfully merged.

00:18:25.100 --> 00:18:28.610
So if we quickly glance at the
build that is going to run,

00:18:28.610 --> 00:18:30.350
we can see here that
it's a single step,

00:18:30.350 --> 00:18:32.780
and we're just using G
Cloud as our build step,

00:18:32.780 --> 00:18:34.490
and we are passing
in the arguments

00:18:34.490 --> 00:18:38.210
to basically deploy to
Cloud Run, our latest image

00:18:38.210 --> 00:18:41.713
that we built. Now let's
take a look at our last

00:18:41.713 --> 00:18:43.130
build from the
second trigger that

00:18:43.130 --> 00:18:47.420
will deploy our new container
image to Google Cloud Run.

00:18:47.420 --> 00:18:51.110
One of the things to consider
is that many teams will often

00:18:51.110 --> 00:18:54.260
introduce a manual approval
stage for pipelines

00:18:54.260 --> 00:18:56.360
that deploy to a
production environment,

00:18:56.360 --> 00:18:59.120
and this basically allows
for an operator or developer

00:18:59.120 --> 00:19:04.310
to have that final decision
to actually promote an image

00:19:04.310 --> 00:19:06.240
into a production environment.

00:19:06.240 --> 00:19:07.910
So as you can see,
Google Cloud Build

00:19:07.910 --> 00:19:11.030
has actually introduced the
notion of manual approvals.

00:19:11.030 --> 00:19:13.710
So we can see that the
trigger has created our build.

00:19:13.710 --> 00:19:15.680
However, the build has
not begun to execute

00:19:15.680 --> 00:19:18.770
to actually deploy to Cloud
Run because I have not yet

00:19:18.770 --> 00:19:20.000
approved it.

00:19:20.000 --> 00:19:22.880
So once we click off Approve,
we may have the ability

00:19:22.880 --> 00:19:25.670
to provide context
on perhaps monitoring

00:19:25.670 --> 00:19:31.430
or certain documentation or a
ticket that actually captures

00:19:31.430 --> 00:19:33.290
whatever metrics or
heuristics that allow

00:19:33.290 --> 00:19:35.720
us to promote this production.

00:19:35.720 --> 00:19:38.210
And we can click
Approve, and our

00:19:38.210 --> 00:19:42.350
build will actually begin
to kick off and run.

00:19:42.350 --> 00:19:43.850
And after a short
period of time,

00:19:43.850 --> 00:19:46.610
we'll see that our single
step build has completed

00:19:46.610 --> 00:19:50.270
and we have now deployed the new
container image to Cloud Run.

00:19:50.270 --> 00:19:52.340
So let's take a
look at Cloud Run.

00:19:52.340 --> 00:19:54.560
And we can actually see
that we have a new revision.

00:19:54.560 --> 00:19:55.935
This was the failed
revision that

00:19:55.935 --> 00:19:59.240
was not signed by the attester
in Google Cloud Build,

00:19:59.240 --> 00:20:02.150
so it failed to deploy due
to Binary Authorization.

00:20:02.150 --> 00:20:04.250
But because we built this
image with Cloud Build

00:20:04.250 --> 00:20:07.580
and it was actually properly
signed by the Cloud Run

00:20:07.580 --> 00:20:11.600
attester that we had, it is
now successfully deployed.

00:20:11.600 --> 00:20:16.890
And once we issue that request
again to our application,

00:20:16.890 --> 00:20:20.780
we can see our changes with the
additional exclamation points.

00:20:20.780 --> 00:20:22.370
We demonstrated
how we can secure

00:20:22.370 --> 00:20:23.870
our build environments
with granular

00:20:23.870 --> 00:20:25.610
build permissions,
a secure network

00:20:25.610 --> 00:20:28.640
perimeter, manual approvals
and admission control

00:20:28.640 --> 00:20:30.950
to targets like
Cloud Run and GKE.

00:20:30.950 --> 00:20:33.730
Back to you, Christopher.

00:20:33.730 --> 00:20:35.890
CHRISTOPHER SANSON:
Thanks, Anthony.

00:20:35.890 --> 00:20:37.190
That was a great demo.

00:20:37.190 --> 00:20:40.240
Now, of course, what we just
covered today was really

00:20:40.240 --> 00:20:43.510
meant as an introduction and
a high level overview of some

00:20:43.510 --> 00:20:45.130
of the specific
actions you can take

00:20:45.130 --> 00:20:48.587
to secure part of your
larger end-to-end ecosystem.

00:20:48.587 --> 00:20:50.170
If you're interested
in learning more,

00:20:50.170 --> 00:20:52.030
here are some
additional talks here

00:20:52.030 --> 00:20:54.040
at Next around securing
your software supply

00:20:54.040 --> 00:20:57.010
chain that we highly recommend
you check out as well.

00:20:57.010 --> 00:20:58.010
Thank you for your time.

00:20:58.010 --> 00:21:00.720
And we're happy to
take any questions.

